  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Monday)...\n\nUpper low along the MT/Saskatchewan border continues to dominate \nour regional weather pattern placing cyclonic flow over the \nRockies and high plains. Shortwave energy rounding the bottom of \nthe flow through southern ID and into northern WY and southern MT \novernight continues to fuel some convection at this time...while a\ncooler northerly push of air has entered our western zones to \nbattle the moist warmer southeast flow across our southeast zones.\nThe associated convergent area in our east seems to be the focus \nof some continual convective development with isolated \nthunderstorms...a few of which may briefly turn stronger due to \nthe effective shear (50 kts). This activity is progged to linger \nthrough the early morning hours before eventually moving into the \nDakotas.\n\nFor today, energy rotating around the upper low will push some \nmoisture and light showers into our CWA from the northwest. This \nwill gradually shift east as the core upper low drifts east. In \nthe meantime, stronger convection may fire along the MT/Dakota \nborders as instability and favorable shear (40-50 kts) provide the\ningredients for potential severe storms (1+\" hail and damaging \nwind). However, the best environment looks to be east of the \nstateline in the Dakotas. Showers and isolated thunder could \nlinger well into the evening and early morning hours across \neastern MT as shortwave forcing affects the region. \n\nMain core upper low shifts to SE Saskatchewan Wednesday placing \ndeeper downslope (westerly) flow over our area. So this looks to \nlimit much if any precipitation aside from a few isolated showers \nover the high country. \n\nAnother shortwave moves across the region Thursday bringing a \ngood chance of showers/storms across our CWA once again. \nSoutheastern Montana has the best chance of seeing severe \nthunderstorms Thursday with significant instability parameters and\neffective shear in place as cold pool associated with shortwave \nmoves across. Low amplitude ridging behind this system should keep\nFriday mostly dry. However, as the next upper trough moves into \nthe Pacific NW, we should see mid level flow back ot the southwest\nover our region and induce strong low level southeast moisture \ninto our region. Any energy ejecting across our area from the \nupper trough may produce a threat of strong thunderstorms at some \npoint this weekend. Details and timing are still uncertain for \nnow. \n\nTemperatures increase throughout the rest of week with 70s \nthrough Thursday reaching into the mid 80s to low 90s by Saturday.\nBT",
